 "verdict": "CONTROL (the CHAOTIC init signature, not a negative one): random init gives lambda +0.3347 with ignition 1.00 and D_norm 0.982, against Pythia's measured +0.3363 at ignition 1.00. It reproduces the chaotic init, as a randomly initialised model must -- damage ignites every time and fills the lattice. PRIMARY: THE DEVELOPMENTAL CURVE'S ENDPOINTS REPLICATE IN A SECOND NON-PYTHIA FAMILY. Init lands at +0.3347 against Pythia's +0.3363, and all 4 trained checkpoints land within 0.1 of Pythia's plateau +0.1683 (measured [0.1593, 0.2087, 0.1767, 0.1755]). With OLMo-2-0425-1B this is two independent families, both measured by dev_transition_phase3.measure imported unchanged -- same estimator, same geometry, only the family varies. lambda_ca across the grid spans 0.1753 against its own noise floor 0.03682 (4.76x, gate 2.0x) BOUNDARY, BINDING ON HOW THIS MAY BE WRITTEN. This replicates the curve's ENDPOINTS, not its TIMING. Pythia's dip spans 0.034-1.07B tokens and NO public non-Pythia family has a checkpoint strictly inside it -- ~4000 branches enumerated across OLMo-1B-0724 (1446, earliest trained 2B), OLMo-2-0425-1B (0B then 1B), OLMo-2-1124-7B, CrystalCoder, K2, and five single-branch repositories. The dip is not observable by anyone today, and that is a fact about the field's checkpoint supply rather than a limitation of this design. ATTRIBUTION: tokenizer, architecture, corpus, data order and optimiser all differ from Pythia simultaneously; this cannot attribute any difference to any one of them.",
